# What NOT to upload to Cloud Build when deploying from source.
# The app that ships is: server.js, package.json, index.html,
# js/, css/, vendor/, models/, LICENSE — minus developer mode's four files,
# named at the bottom of this file.
#
# `lab.html` left that list on 2026-09-03: the dice lab retired with js/lab.js
# and its two shot tools when the developer-mode panel's sets editor took over
# the recipe knobs on the live felt (docs/DEVMODE.md §9).
#
# models/ was missing from that list and it is not a cosmetic omission: the
# baked GLB towers are served from /models/towers/*.glb (js/main.js), so a
# reader checking "is my new asset going to ship?" against this comment
# would have concluded the towers do not.
#
# THIS FILE IS THE WHOLE RULE WHEN IT EXISTS. gcloud only falls back to
# .gitignore when there is no .gcloudignore, so nothing gitignored is
# excluded here unless it is named below.

.git
.gitignore
.gcloudignore
.claude/
docs/
tests/
tools/
deploy/
Makefile
CLAUDE.md
README.md
chrome-lab.html

# Measurement and capture leftovers. Neither is part of the app and both
# were riding into the container: gpu-trace.csv is the raw nvidia-smi trace
# behind the DICE_E2E_CORES cap (commit 9584fde) and belongs with the
# reasoning, not with the server; shots/ is the local screenshot dump, which
# is gitignored and therefore invisible to a check that trusts .gitignore.
gpu-trace.csv
shots/

# DEVELOPER MODE IS NOT PART OF THE DEPLOYED APP (docs/DEVMODE.md §8, phase 3).
# `app.mode: production` already makes the door refuse — but that is a value in
# a file, and a value can be edited by whoever can reach the file. These four
# are the SECOND answer, the one that does not depend on a setting: the panel,
# its component kit, its stylesheet and the pop-out page are not uploaded at
# all, so the deploy has no bytes to open. `tools/` is excluded above, so
# tools/devshell.html goes with it, and `js/tune.js` deliberately does NOT: the
# declaration is what the shipped app is configured by, not dev chrome.
#
# js/main.js imports js/devmode.js DYNAMICALLY and nowhere else, and `devOpen`
# latches on the import's failure (one console warn, the door stays shut, the
# backtick stops being a key) — the `dev-absent-in-prod` scenario boots a tab
# from a tree with exactly these four files missing and proves it, /dev.html's
# own 404 included.
js/devmode.js
js/devui.js
css/dev.css
# …and the fourth: the pop-out page that mounts the panel in its own window
# (phase D5). It imports js/devmode.js, so on a deploy it would 404 on its own
# import even if it were uploaded; withholding the page as well keeps
# `/dev.html` from being a 200 that leads nowhere.
dev.html
